: {"x": 23.04690402476781, "y": 11.900000000000006, "width": 71.25309597523223, "height": 41.100000000000016, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction-changed"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 23.04690402476781, "y": 11.900000000000006, "width": 71.25309597523223, "height": 41.100000000000016, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction-changed"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 23.04690402476781, "y": 11.900000000000006, "width": 71.25309597523223, "height": 41.100000000000016, "rotation": 0, "text": ["Scientists typically say that Planck launched a new \"quantum revolution\" with his work in 1900. In modern derivations, we arrive at Planck's expression for u(v,T) by stipulating that the energy exchanged between matter and radiation must be quantized, in units E = h v, rather than treated as continuous. Is that what Planck thought be was doing? \nKuhn: In Planck's 1900 derivation, he fixed the total energy of the system to be an integer number of units of h\u03bd,"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction-changed"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 44.8, "y": 55, "width": 21.9, "height": 10.2, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 44.8, "y": 55, "width": 21.9, "height": 10.2, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["equation"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 44.8, "y": 55, "width": 21.9, "height": 10.2, "rotation": 0, "text": ["$$\nE _ { \\mathrm { t o t a l } } = \\sum _ { i } E _ { i } = N h \\nu\n$$"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 3.1, "y": 65.5, "width": 91.4, "height": 15.8, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 3.1, "y": 65.5, "width": 91.4, "height": 15.8, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 3.1, "y": 65.5, "width": 91.4, "height": 15.8, "rotation": 0, "text": ["but he did not require E_i = h\u03bd. In fact, in Planck's description, the energies for sub-systems fell \"in the range (E_i, E_i + \u0394E_i).\" Planck used bins of size \u03b5 = h\u03bd for his accounting, but then counted how many oscillators of continuous energy fell within bins [0, \u03b5], [\u03b5, 2\u03b5], and so on."]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 4.2, "y": 85.5, "width": 92.5, "height": 5, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_6", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 4.2, "y": 85.5, "width": 92.5, "height": 5, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_6", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 640, "original_height": 360, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 4.2, "y": 85.5, "width": 92.5, "height": 5, "rotation": 0, "text": ["I